  <div id="lessonContent">
    <h2>1. What is an API?</h2>
    <p><b>API = A Waiter in a Restaurant.</b> You (Website) tell the waiter what you want - "Weather of Lahore". The waiter goes to the kitchen (Server), gets the food (Data), and brings it to you. You never go to the kitchen yourself.</p>
    <p>Example: When you open a Weather App and type "Karachi", your app asks OpenWeatherMap API - "Give me Karachi weather". The API sends back JSON data - Temperature, Humidity, Wind. Your app shows it on screen.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>Real Life:</b> Google Maps API = Maps data. YouTube API = Videos data. Weather API = Weather data. News API = News data. Every big app you use is made of APIs.</div>
    <h2>2. LIVE DEMO - Weather App (Demo Data - Works Without API Key)</h2>
    <p>Try cities - Lahore, Karachi, Islamabad, London, New York, Dubai. In real app, this data comes from API. Here we use demo data so it works without API key:</p>
    <div class="demo-card">
      <div class="weather-wrap">
        <h3 style="margin:0">🌤️ Weather App</h3>
        <p style="font-size:13px;opacity:.9">Type city name or click a city below</p>
        <div style="display:flex;gap:8px;margin-top:10px">
          <input class="demo-input" id="cityInput" type="text" placeholder="Type city - e.g. Lahore" style="margin:0" onkeydown="if(event.key==='Enter') searchWeather()">
          <button class="btn btn-js" style="margin:0;white-space:nowrap" onclick="searchWeather()">Search 🔍</button>
        </div>
